# @@NAME@@

A [collage](https://collage.furkanbaytekin.dev/en/) project, scaffolded by
`collage new`. The guide starts at
https://collage.furkanbaytekin.dev/en/docs/introduction/, and the Go reference is
at https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/Elagoht/collage/pkg/collage.

## Running

```
go mod tidy
cp .env.example .env.development
collage dev
```

The site is at http://localhost:6060. `collage dev` reads `.env.development`
(or `.env` when there is none) and runs the project in development mode:
templates and static files are read from disk on every request, and the page
reloads itself when one changes. A change to Go code is rebuilt and restarted for you;
one that does not compile leaves the last good build running and says why.

A variable set in your shell wins over the file, so `PORT=4000 collage dev`
still works. The program reads them too: `go run .` and the built binary load
`.env` from the directory they run in (`.env.development` first in development),
so `os.Getenv` sees the same values everywhere without a `source .env`.

```
go test ./...
```

## What is here

```
main.go       newApp: configuration, plugins and the static mount; collage.Main runs it
routes.go     every page, document and action — a new route goes here
main_test.go  the tests, driving app.Handler() with pkg/collagetest
pages/        a page per file, by area: pages/demo/hello.go is the hello page
fragments/    layouts/, and pages/: each page's content, mirroring pages/
actions/      POST and friends, by domain; their handlers in actions/funcs/
documents/    routes that are not HTML, like /healthz
data/         state, by domain: the demo's in-memory count
templates/    the HTML kept in files; small fragments keep theirs inline
static/       CSS, JavaScript and images, at /static/
```

`/` is the home page, declared `Static()`, so `collage export` writes it to a
file. `/features` has four live demos:

- **API** — a button posts to `/api/count`, an action that answers with JSON
  and invalidates the cached page by tag.
- **Forms** — a plain HTML form posts to `/hello`, and the page's own action
  renders the greeting.
- **Fragment URLs** — the clock is a fragment opened at `/fragments/clock` with
  `WithFragmentPath`, fetched and swapped in without a client framework.
- **Documents** — `/healthz` is JSON, routed like a page but never passed
  through HTML.

## Adding to it

`collage add` writes a page, a fragment, an action, a document or a guard in
this layout, and registers it in `routes.go`:

```
collage add page blog/post          # pages/blog/post.go + fragments/pages/blog/post.go
collage add page blog/post --file   # the template in templates/pages/blog/post.html
collage add fragment blog/sidebar   # fragments/blog/sidebar.go, for a slot
collage add action blog/comment     # actions/blog.go + actions/funcs/blog.go
collage add document feed --path /feed.xml --type application/xml
collage add guard admin             # guards/admin.go + a layout that carries it
collage add page admin/users --guard admin
```

A new guard refuses everyone until you write its check. `collage help add` has
the rest.

## Set `COLLAGE_CSRF_KEY` before deploying

It signs the tokens `{{csrfToken}}` puts in forms. Without one, collage
generates a key per process — fine while developing, where it is only
mentioned, and wrong in production, where it warns: every form submitted before
a restart is refused after it, and one instance refuses what another issued.

```
openssl rand -hex 32
```

Put the result in `.env.development` for development, and in your production
environment's secrets for the deployed binary.

## Deploying

There is no `collage start`: this is a Go program, so production is a binary.

```
collage build              # -> bin/@@NAME@@, built for linux/amd64
collage build -i           # also offers a Dockerfile and a systemd unit in bin/
HOST=0.0.0.0 PORT=8080 COLLAGE_CSRF_KEY=... ./bin/@@NAME@@
```

Templates and static files are embedded (`embed.go`), so the binary is the whole site —
nothing to copy next to it. It drains in-flight requests on `SIGTERM`, answers
`/healthz` for a liveness check, and caches rendered pages under `.cache` so a
restart does not re-render everything. If you add the elagoht/health plugin, delete
`documents/health.go` or move the plugin's `livePath`: its middleware answers
`/healthz` first, so the document would never be reached.

`collage export` writes the static pages to `dist/` for any static host. Pages
that need a server — a form, or a `Dynamic()` page — are skipped and named, and
still work when the binary serves them.

## Plugins

What the framework leaves out comes as plugins, each its own module. A few worth
adding early:

- [collage-htmlcheck](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-htmlcheck) checks the
  HTML you render — a missing `alt`, a skipped heading, a link to nothing — over the
  page in development and in `collage export`'s report.
- [collage-sitemap](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-sitemap),
  [collage-robots](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-robots) and
  [collage-feed](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-feed) for search engines and
  feed readers.
- [collage-secure](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-secure) for security headers,
  [collage-flash](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-flash) for a "saved" message
  after a form, [collage-i18n](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage-i18n) for
  translations.

`collage plugin add elagoht/sitemap` fetches one, adds it to `plugins.go`, and
gives it an empty section in `plugins-config.json`, where its settings go;
`collage plugin del` takes it out again. All of them:
https://collage.furkanbaytekin.dev/en/docs/plugins/.

## The collage CLI contract

`main.go` is one line, `collage.Main(newApp)`, and `newApp` builds the
application from the `collage.Env` it is given. `collage.Main` is what the
`collage` binary talks to:

- **`collage dev`** builds this program with `go build -tags collage_dev` —
  which leaves out `embed.go`, so development builds embed nothing and read
  `templates/` and `static/` from disk — and runs it with `COLLAGE_DEV=1`, which
  `collage.Main` passes on as `env.Dev`. The browser talks to `collage dev`,
  which passes requests on to the program at the `HOST` and `PORT` it sets for
  it, and rebuilds and restarts it when its Go code changes.
- **`collage export`** runs `go run . -collage-build -out <dir>`;
  `collage.Main` renders through `collage.Build`, collage's own builder.

Keep `newApp` building everything — configuration, plugins, routes, commands —
and leave the flags to `collage.Main`, or `collage dev` and `collage export` stop
doing anything useful here.

Commands — a plugin's, or your own registered with `app.RegisterCommand` in
`newApp` — run through this program rather than the `collage` binary, which
never loads your code: `go run . <command>`, or `./bin/@@NAME@@ <command>` on a
server. `collage.Main` runs the first word after its flags as a command instead
of serving, and shuts every plugin down when it is done.
